29	8	Test Case Generation from User Story	ROLE:\nYou are a senior QA Engineer specializing in Native iOS and Android mobile application testing.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nStory Title: [Story Title]\nStory ID: [JIRA ID]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Both\nBuild Version: [Version Number or "Not yet available"]\nTesting Type: Functional | Regression | Smoke | Exploratory\n\nTASK:\nGenerate exhaustive test cases for the user story and acceptance criteria provided below.\nYou MUST evaluate ALL of the following categories — do not skip any:\n  - Happy path (primary success flow)\n  - Negative / error paths (invalid input, wrong state, network failure)\n  - Edge cases (boundary values, empty states, maximum limits)\n  - Platform-specific behavior (iOS vs Android differences)\n  - Accessibility (screen reader, font scaling, contrast)\n\nEach test case must be fully independent and executable without referencing another test case.\nEvaluate the ENTIRE story and ALL acceptance criteria before writing any test case.\nDo NOT skip negative or edge case scenarios — these are as important as happy path.\n\nUSER STORY & ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA:\n[Paste story and AC here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Test cases must be written so a junior tester can execute them without clarification\n- All preconditions must be listed explicitly — assume nothing\n- Platform differences between iOS and Android must be called out in their own test cases\n- Do not group multiple verifications into one expected result — one check per test case\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nTEST CASE [N]:\nTitle: [Short descriptive title]\nType: Happy Path | Negative | Edge Case | Accessibility | Platform-Specific\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Both\nPreconditions:\n  - [Setup requirement 1]\n  - [Setup requirement 2]\nSteps:\n  1. [Step]\n  2. [Step]\n  3. [Step]\nExpected Result: [Exact, observable outcome]\nNotes: [iOS/Android difference, known risk, or "None"]\n\n---\n(repeat for every test case)\n\nSUMMARY:\nTotal Test Cases: [N]\nHappy Path: [N] | Negative: [N] | Edge Case: [N] | Accessibility: [N] | Platform-Specific: [N]\nCoverage Gaps: [Topics not covered due to missing AC — or "None"]
30	8	Bug Report Drafting	ROLE:\nYou are a QA Engineer writing a formal bug report for a Native iOS/Android mobile application.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nApp Version: [Version]\nDevice Model: [e.g., iPhone 15 Pro | Samsung Galaxy S24]\nOS Version: [e.g., iOS 17.4 | Android 14]\nEnvironment: Dev | QA | Staging | Production\nBuild Number: [Build #]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the raw observation below, produce a complete, developer-ready bug report.\nSteps to reproduce must be precise enough for a developer to replicate on first attempt.\nClassify severity and priority independently — a cosmetic bug can be high priority\nif it affects a release deadline.\nIf information needed to complete a field is missing, mark it [NEEDS CAPTURE] — never leave blank.\n\nRAW OBSERVATION:\n[Describe what you saw — can be informal]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Steps to reproduce must be numbered and atomic — one action per step, never compound\n- Do not include opinion or blame — only observable, factual statements\n- Actual Result must describe exactly what happened, not what you think caused it\n- Reproducibility must be based on actual attempts — do not guess\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nBUG REPORT\nTitle: [Clear searchable title — what broke + where + on what platform]\nSeverity: Critical | High | Medium | Low\nPriority: High | Medium | Low\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Both\nStatus: New\n\nEnvironment:\n  App Version: [Version] | Build: [#] | Device: [Model] | OS: [Version] | Env: [Dev/QA/Staging/Prod]\n\nSteps to Reproduce:\n  1. [Step]\n  2. [Step]\n  3. [Step]\n  ...\n\nExpected Result: [What should have happened]\nActual Result: [What actually happened — observable fact only]\nReproducibility: Always | Intermittent ([N] of [N] attempts) | Once\n\nAttachments: [Screenshot / Video / Logs — or "Pending capture"]\nWorkaround: [Known workaround if any — or "None"]\nRelated Tickets: [Linked JIRA IDs — or "None"]\nNotes: [Any additional context — or "None"]
32	8	Regression Suite Prioritization	ROLE:\nYou are a senior QA Engineer prioritizing a regression test suite for a\nNative iOS/Android mobile application release.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nRelease Version: [Version]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Both\nRelease Type: Major | Minor | Hotfix | Patch\nTime Available for Regression: [e.g., 2 days | 1 sprint | 4 hours]\nRelease Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the release scope and existing test suite provided below, prioritize regression\ntests into three tiers: Must Run, Should Run, and Can Skip.\nBase prioritization on: areas changed in this release, user-facing critical paths,\nhistorical defect hotspots, and platform-specific risks.\nEvaluate the ENTIRE test suite before assigning tiers — do not assign on first pass.\nFlag any area of the app changed in this release that has NO regression test coverage.\n\nRELEASE SCOPE (stories/changes in this release):\n[Paste list of stories, PRs, or change descriptions here]\n\nEXISTING REGRESSION TEST SUITE:\n[Paste test case IDs, titles, and areas they cover]\n\nHISTORICAL DEFECT AREAS (if known):\n[Paste known bug-prone areas or "Not available"]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Must Run tier must be executable within the time available stated above\n- Do not assign Must Run to tests unrelated to this release's changes\n- Every changed area must have at least one Must Run test — flag if it doesn't\n- Platform-specific tests must be tiered separately for iOS and Android\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nREGRESSION PRIORITIZATION REPORT\nRelease: [Version] | Platform: [iOS/Android/Both] | Time Budget: [X days/hours]\nGenerated: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\n🔴 MUST RUN ([N] tests — Est. [X hours]):\n| Test ID | Test Title | Area | Platform | Reason for Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| TC-001 | [Title] | [Feature area] | iOS | [Changed in this release] |\n\n🟡 SHOULD RUN IF TIME PERMITS ([N] tests — Est. [X hours]):\n| Test ID | Test Title | Area | Platform | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n🟢 CAN SKIP THIS RELEASE ([N] tests):\n| Test ID | Test Title | Reason for Skip |\n|---|---|---|\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (changed areas with no regression test):\n| Changed Area | Story ID | Risk | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Area] | [ID] | High/Med/Low | [Create new test / Manual exploratory] |\n\nSUMMARY:\nTotal Suite Size: [N] | Must Run: [N] | Should Run: [N] | Skipped: [N]\nEstimated Must Run Duration: [X hours] | Fits Time Budget: Yes | No
34	9	Framework design prompts	ROLE:\nYou are a senior Automation Test Engineer specializing in designing\ntest automation frameworks for Native iOS, Android, and web applications.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Web | All\nTechnology Stack: [e.g., React Native | Swift | Kotlin | Node.js]\nTeam Size: [Number of automation engineers who will use this framework]\nExisting Automation: None | Partial — [describe what exists] | Full migration\nCI/CD Platform: GitHub Actions | Bitrise | Jenkins | GitLab CI | Fastlane\nTest Management Tool: [e.g., TestRail | Zephyr | JIRA | None]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the project details and requirements provided below, design a complete\ntest automation framework architecture.\n\nYou MUST cover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Framework pattern — Page Object Model, Screenplay, Data-Driven, Keyword-Driven, or Hybrid\n  2. Folder and file structure — complete directory tree with purpose of each folder\n  3. Core components — base classes, utilities, helpers, config management\n  4. Locator strategy — how element locators are stored, named, and maintained\n  5. Test data management — how test data is stored, injected, and kept environment-agnostic\n  6. Reporting — tool, format, and what is captured per test run\n  7. CI integration — how the framework plugs into the CI/CD pipeline\n  8. Parallel execution strategy — how tests run in parallel without conflict\n  9. Dependency and package management — tools and versioning approach\n  10. Onboarding — what a new engineer needs to do to run their first test\n\nEvaluate the FULL project context before designing any component.\nEvery design decision must include a brief justification — do not list options without recommending one.\nIf critical information is missing, flag it as [NEEDS DECISION] — never assume.\n\nPROJECT REQUIREMENTS AND CONSTRAINTS:\n[Paste project details, tech stack, team constraints, and any existing tooling here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Framework must be maintainable by a team of the size stated above — do not over-engineer\n- All recommendations must be compatible with the platform and CI tool stated in CONTEXT\n- Do not recommend deprecated or unmaintained libraries\n- Every component in the folder structure must have a clearly stated purpose\n- Parallel execution design must address test isolation explicitly — not just "use threading"\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nAUTOMATION FRAMEWORK DESIGN\nProject: [Project Name] | Platform: [Platform] | Pattern: [Chosen Pattern]\nPrepared By: [Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\n---\n\n1. FRAMEWORK PATTERN:\nRecommended: [Pattern Name]\nJustification: [Why this pattern fits this project specifically]\n\n---\n\n2. FOLDER STRUCTURE:\n[Root]/\n  [folder]/         -- [Purpose]\n    [subfolder]/    -- [Purpose]\n  [folder]/         -- [Purpose]\n(show full tree, every folder explained)\n\n---\n\n3. CORE COMPONENTS:\n| Component | File/Class Name | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Base Test Class | [Name] | [Purpose] |\n| Config Manager | [Name] | [Purpose] |\n| Driver Factory | [Name] | [Purpose] |\n| Custom Wait Utility | [Name] | [Purpose] |\n| Logger | [Name] | [Purpose] |\n\n---\n\n4. LOCATOR STRATEGY:\nApproach: [e.g., Centralised Page Object per screen | Separate locator files]\nNaming Convention: [e.g., btn_login | txt_username]\nStorage Format: [e.g., constants in class | external JSON/YAML]\nPlatform Handling: [How iOS vs Android locators are separated]\n\n---\n\n5. TEST DATA MANAGEMENT:\nStorage: [e.g., JSON files | YAML | Database | Faker library]\nInjection Method: [e.g., parameterised tests | fixture files | factory classes]\nEnvironment Switching: [How data changes between Dev / QA / Staging]\nSensitive Data: [How credentials and PII are handled — never hardcoded]\n\n---\n\n6. REPORTING:\nTool: [e.g., Allure | ExtentReports | Built-in framework reporter]\nFormat: HTML | JSON | XML | All\nCaptured Per Run: Pass/Fail counts, screenshots on failure, execution time, environment\nIntegration: [How reports are published in CI — e.g., artifact upload, Slack notification]\n\n---\n\n7. CI INTEGRATION:\nTrigger: [e.g., On PR | On merge to main | Nightly scheduled]\nPipeline Steps:\n  1. [Step — e.g., Checkout code]\n  2. [Step — e.g., Install dependencies]\n  3. [Step — e.g., Start emulator / simulator]\n  4. [Step — e.g., Run test suite]\n  5. [Step — e.g., Publish report]\nConfig File: [e.g., .github/workflows/automation.yml | Jenkinsfile]\n\n---\n\n8. PARALLEL EXECUTION:\nStrategy: [e.g., Test class level | Test method level]\nIsolation Approach: [How each parallel thread gets its own driver instance]\nDevice Handling: [How multiple devices / simulators are managed in parallel]\nMax Parallel Threads: [Recommended number based on team size and CI resources]\n\n---\n\n9. DEPENDENCY MANAGEMENT:\nTool: [e.g., Gradle | Maven | npm | pip | Swift Package Manager]\nKey Dependencies:\n| Library | Version | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Library] | [Version or "Latest stable"] | [Purpose] |\n\n---\n\n10. ONBOARDING STEPS:\nNew engineer setup — from zero to first test run:\n  1. [Step]\n  2. [Step]\n  3. [Step]\n  ...\n\n---\n\nDECISIONS NEEDED:\n| Item | Options | Recommendation | Decision By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Item] | [Option A / Option B] | [Recommended] | [Role — e.g., QA Lead] |\n| [Any [NEEDS DECISION] items flagged above] | | | |
35	9	CI integration guidance	ROLE:\nYou are a senior Automation Test Engineer specializing in CI/CD pipeline\nintegration for Native iOS, Android, and web test automation.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Web | All\nCI/CD Tool: GitHub Actions | Bitrise | Jenkins | GitLab CI | CircleCI | Fastlane\nAutomation Framework: XCUITest | Espresso | Appium | Playwright | Cypress | Selenium\nRepository Host: GitHub | GitLab | Bitbucket\nDeployment Target: App Store | Google Play | Web Server | Internal Distribution\nEnvironment: Dev | QA | Staging | Production\nTeam Size: [Number of engineers committing to this pipeline]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the project details and current pipeline state provided below, produce a\ncomplete CI integration plan for the test automation suite.\n\nYou MUST cover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Pipeline trigger strategy — when tests run and why\n  2. Pipeline stages — every stage from code commit to test report, in order\n  3. Environment and device provisioning — simulators, emulators, or real device farms\n  4. Secrets and credentials management — how sensitive values are stored and injected\n  5. Test splitting and parallelisation — how to reduce total pipeline execution time\n  6. Failure handling — what happens when tests fail, flaky test strategy\n  7. Reporting and notifications — where results go and who is notified\n  8. Artefact management — what is stored after each run and for how long\n  9. Branch strategy — which branches trigger which test suites\n  10. Pipeline configuration file — ready-to-use config for the CI tool stated above\n\nEvaluate the FULL project context before designing any pipeline stage.\nEvery stage must have a clear purpose — do not add stages without justification.\nIf critical information is missing, flag it as [NEEDS DECISION] — never assume.\n\nCURRENT PIPELINE STATE AND REQUIREMENTS:\n[Paste existing pipeline config, pain points, or "No pipeline exists yet" here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Pipeline must complete within a reasonable time for the trigger type — specify target duration\n- Secrets must NEVER be hardcoded in any pipeline config file — flag if found\n- Flaky test strategy must be explicit — do not just say "retry failed tests" without parameters\n- Device provisioning must match the platform stated above — do not suggest iOS simulators for Android\n- Config file produced must be valid syntax for the CI tool stated in CONTEXT\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nCI INTEGRATION PLAN\nProject: [Project Name] | Platform: [Platform] | CI Tool: [Tool]\nPrepared By: [Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\n---\n\n1. PIPELINE TRIGGER STRATEGY:\n| Trigger | Branch / Event | Test Suite Triggered | Justification |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pull Request opened | feature/* | Smoke suite | Fast feedback before merge |\n| Merge to main | main | Full regression suite | Gate before release |\n| Nightly scheduled | main | Full suite + performance | Catch regressions overnight |\n| [Additional trigger] | [Branch] | [Suite] | [Reason] |\n\n---\n\n2. PIPELINE STAGES:\n| Stage | Purpose | Tool / Command | Est. Duration | On Failure |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Checkout | Clone repository | git checkout | <1 min | Abort |\n| Install Dependencies | Install packages | [e.g., npm install / gradle build] | [X min] | Abort |\n| Lint & Static Analysis | Code quality gate | [Tool] | [X min] | Warn / Abort |\n| Build | Compile app binary | [e.g., xcodebuild / gradlew] | [X min] | Abort |\n| Provision Devices | Start simulators/emulators | [Command] | [X min] | Abort |\n| Run Tests | Execute automation suite | [Command] | [X min] | Continue + Report |\n| Publish Report | Upload test results | [Tool] | <1 min | Warn |\n| Notify | Send pass/fail notification | [e.g., Slack / Email] | <1 min | Never abort |\n\n---\n\n3. ENVIRONMENT AND DEVICE PROVISIONING:\niOS:\n  Simulator: [e.g., iPhone 15 Pro — iOS 17 — xcodebuild -destination]\n  Real Device Farm: [e.g., BrowserStack / AWS Device Farm / Firebase Test Lab — or "Not used"]\n\nAndroid:\n  Emulator: [e.g., AVD — API 34 — emulator -avd]\n  Real Device Farm: [e.g., BrowserStack / Firebase Test Lab — or "Not used"]\n\nEnvironment Variables Required:\n  - DEVICE_UDID: [Purpose]\n  - APP_BUILD_PATH: [Purpose]\n  - [Additional env vars]\n\n---\n\n4. SECRETS AND CREDENTIALS MANAGEMENT:\n| Secret | Storage Location | Injection Method | Rotation Policy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| App signing certificate | [e.g., GitHub Secrets / Vault] | Environment variable | [e.g., Annually] |\n| Device farm API key | [e.g., GitHub Secrets] | Environment variable | [e.g., Quarterly] |\n| Test user credentials | [e.g., GitHub Secrets] | Environment variable | [e.g., Per release] |\n| [Additional secret] | | | |\n\nRule: No secret may appear in any committed file — pipeline must fail if detected.\n\n---\n\n5. TEST SPLITTING AND PARALLELISATION:\nStrategy: [e.g., Split by test class | Split by feature tag | Split by estimated duration]\nParallel Jobs: [N] — Justification: [Why this number]\nIsolation: [How each parallel job gets its own device / driver instance]\nMerge Results: [How parallel job results are combined into one report]\nEst. Time Saving: [Sequential duration] → [Parallel duration]\n\n---\n\n6. FAILURE HANDLING:\n| Scenario | Action | Notification |\n|---|---|---|\n| Single test fails | Mark suite as failed; continue remaining tests | QA Engineer |\n| >20% of suite fails | Abort pipeline; block merge | QA Lead + Developer |\n| Build stage fails | Abort all stages immediately | Developer |\n| Flaky test detected | Retry max [N] times; flag as flaky in report if still failing | QA Lead |\n\nFlaky Test Policy:\n  Definition: Fails in [N] of [N] consecutive runs without code change\n  Action: Quarantine tag applied; removed from blocking suite; tracked in [Tool]\n  Resolution SLA: Must be fixed or permanently removed within [N] sprints\n\n---\n\n7. REPORTING AND NOTIFICATIONS:\nReport Tool: [e.g., Allure | ExtentReports | Native CI report]\nReport Published To: [e.g., GitHub Pages | S3 | Confluence | CI artifact]\nRetention: [e.g., Last 30 runs | 90 days]\n\nNotifications:\n| Event | Channel | Recipients |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pipeline pass | Slack #qa-alerts | QA Team |\n| Pipeline fail | Slack #qa-alerts + Email | QA Lead + Dev who triggered |\n| Flaky test detected | Slack #qa-alerts | QA Lead |\n\n---\n\n8. ARTEFACT MANAGEMENT:\n| Artefact | When Captured | Storage | Retention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Test report (HTML) | Every run | CI artefact storage | 30 days |\n| Screenshots on failure | On test failure | CI artefact storage | 30 days |\n| Video recording | On test failure | CI artefact storage | 14 days |\n| App binary (.ipa/.apk) | On build stage | CI artefact storage | 7 days |\n| Logs | Every run | CI artefact storage | 14 days |\n\n---\n\n9. BRANCH STRATEGY:\n| Branch Pattern | Suite | Blocking? | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| feature/* | Smoke only | Yes — blocks PR merge | Fast feedback |\n| develop | Smoke + Regression | Yes — blocks merge to main | Full validation |\n| main | Full suite + Accessibility | Yes — blocks release | Release gate |\n| hotfix/* | Smoke + targeted regression | Yes | Expedited path |\n\n---\n\n10. PIPELINE CONFIGURATION FILE:\nCI Tool: [Tool stated in CONTEXT]\nFile Location: [e.g., .github/workflows/automation.yml | Jenkinsfile | bitrise.yml]\n\n[Ready-to-use pipeline config file for the CI tool — valid syntax, no placeholders left blank]\n\n---\n\nDECISIONS NEEDED:\n| Item | Options | Recommendation | Decision By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Any [NEEDS DECISION] items] | [Option A / Option B] | [Recommended option] | [Role] |
36	10	Advanced test scenario design	ROLE:\nYou are a senior SDET (Software Development Engineer in Test) specializing in\nadvanced test scenario design for Native iOS, Android, and web applications.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Web | All\nFeature / Module Being Tested: [Feature or module name]\nTesting Level: System | Integration | End-to-End | Contract\nRisk Level: High | Medium | Low\nSprint / Release: [Sprint number or release version]\nDependencies: [External APIs, third-party services, microservices involved — or "None"]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the feature description, architecture context, and risk profile provided below,\ndesign advanced test scenarios that go beyond basic happy path coverage.\n\nYou MUST evaluate ALL of the following categories — do not skip any:\n  1. State transition scenarios — system behaviour across multiple states and state changes\n  2. Concurrency and race conditions — simultaneous actions by multiple users or processes\n  3. Integration boundary scenarios — failures and degraded responses at external dependencies\n  4. Data integrity scenarios — correctness of data across service boundaries and persistence layers\n  5. Security scenarios — authentication, authorisation, injection, and data exposure risks\n  6. Performance boundary scenarios — behaviour at expected load limits and just beyond\n  7. Recovery scenarios — system behaviour after crash, network loss, or partial failure\n  8. Platform-specific scenarios — OS version differences, device capability variations\n\nEvaluate the FULL feature and architecture context before designing any scenario.\nEach scenario must be independent and testable without relying on another scenario's outcome.\nDo NOT limit scenarios to what is easy to automate — design for risk, then decide automation feasibility.\nIf architecture detail needed to design a scenario is missing, flag it as [NEEDS CLARIFICATION].\n\nFEATURE DESCRIPTION AND ARCHITECTURE CONTEXT:\n[Paste feature description, system architecture, API contracts, and data flow here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Each scenario must specify the exact system state required before execution\n- Integration scenarios must define the exact failure mode being simulated — not just "API fails"\n- Security scenarios must reference a specific threat — not generic "security testing"\n- Performance boundary scenarios must state the specific threshold being tested\n- Do not duplicate scenarios — each must test a distinct behaviour or failure mode\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nADVANCED TEST SCENARIO DESIGN\nFeature: [Feature Name] | Platform: [Platform] | Risk Level: [High/Med/Low]\nPrepared By: [Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\n---\n\nSCENARIO [N]:\nTitle: [Short descriptive title]\nCategory: State Transition | Concurrency | Integration Boundary | Data Integrity |\n          Security | Performance Boundary | Recovery | Platform-Specific\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Web | All\nRisk: High | Medium | Low\nAutomation Feasibility: High | Medium | Low | Manual Only\nReason if Manual Only: [Why this cannot be automated — or "N/A"]\n\nPreconditions:\n  - [Exact system state required]\n  - [Data setup required]\n  - [External dependency state — e.g., "Payment API returns 503"]\n\nScenario Steps:\n  1. [Action]\n  2. [Action]\n  3. [Action]\n\nExpected Outcome: [Exact, observable, measurable result]\nRisk if Not Tested: [What could go wrong in production if this scenario is skipped]\nAutomation Approach: [Tool / method to automate — or "Manual exploratory"]\nNotes: [Architecture assumption, dependency, or known complexity — or "None"]\n\n---\n(repeat for every scenario)\n\nSCENARIO SUMMARY:\n| Category | Count | High Risk | Automatable |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| State Transition | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n| Concurrency | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n| Integration Boundary | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n| Data Integrity | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n| Security | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n| Performance Boundary | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n| Recovery | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n| Platform-Specific | [N] | [N] | [N] |\n\nTotal Scenarios: [N] | High Risk: [N] | Automatable: [N] | Manual Only: [N]\nCoverage Gaps: [Areas not covered due to missing architecture detail — or "None"]
37	10	Test data generation	ROLE:\nYou are a senior SDET specializing in test data generation and management\nfor Native iOS, Android, and web applications.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Web | All\nEnvironment: Dev | QA | Staging | All\nData Type: User accounts | Transaction records | Product catalogues |\n           API payloads | Database records | File uploads | All\nAutomation Framework: [Framework being used — e.g., Appium | Playwright | Espresso]\nDatabase: PostgreSQL | MySQL | MongoDB | SQLite | None\nCompliance Requirements: GDPR | HIPAA | PCI-DSS | None\n\nTASK:\nUsing the feature description and data requirements provided below, produce a\ncomplete test data generation strategy and ready-to-use data assets.\n\nYou MUST cover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Data categories — every distinct type of data needed for this feature's test coverage\n  2. Boundary and edge case data — empty, null, maximum length, special characters, unicode\n  3. Negative data — invalid formats, out-of-range values, injected malicious strings\n  4. Relational data — data sets that span multiple entities with correct foreign key relationships\n  5. Environment-specific data — how data differs between Dev, QA, and Staging\n  6. Sensitive data handling — masking, anonymisation, and synthetic generation rules\n  7. Data generation method — static fixtures, factory classes, Faker library, or database seeding\n  8. Data teardown — how generated data is cleaned up after test execution\n\nEvaluate the FULL feature requirements before generating any data set.\nEvery data set must be directly traceable to a test scenario or acceptance criterion.\nDo NOT use real production data — all data must be synthetic or anonymised.\nIf schema or field validation rules are missing, flag as [NEEDS CLARIFICATION].\n\nFEATURE DESCRIPTION AND DATA REQUIREMENTS:\n[Paste feature description, data schema, field validation rules, and AC here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Sensitive fields (passwords, PII, payment data) must never appear as plaintext in fixtures\n- Boundary values must be mathematically precise — not approximate\n- Relational data sets must maintain referential integrity across all entities\n- Data teardown strategy must not affect other parallel test runs\n- All generated data must be reproducible — use seeded random generation, not pure random\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nTEST DATA GENERATION PLAN\nFeature: [Feature Name] | Platform: [Platform] | Environment: [Env]\nPrepared By: [Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\n\n---\n\n1. DATA CATEGORIES:\n| Category | Description | Volume Needed | Generation Method |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Valid user accounts] | [Description] | [N records] | [Factory / Faker / Static] |\n| [e.g., Invalid email formats] | [Description] | [N records] | [Static fixture] |\n\n---\n\n2. BOUNDARY AND EDGE CASE DATA:\n| Field | Min Value | Max Value | Empty/Null | Special Characters | Unicode |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Field name] | [Value] | [Value] | Allowed / Not Allowed | [Examples] | [Yes/No] |\n\n---\n\n3. NEGATIVE DATA SET:\n| Scenario | Field | Invalid Value | Expected System Response |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., SQL injection attempt] | [username] | [' OR 1=1 --] | [Reject with 400 / sanitise] |\n| [e.g., Oversized input] | [bio] | [5001 chars] | [Reject — max 5000] |\n\n---\n\n4. RELATIONAL DATA SET:\nEntity Map:\n  [Parent Entity] (1) ──── (N) [Child Entity]\n  [e.g., User] (1) ──── (N) [Orders] ──── (N) [Order Items]\n\nSample Relational Fixture:\n  [Show a minimal but complete set of related records maintaining referential integrity]\n\n---\n\n5. ENVIRONMENT-SPECIFIC DATA:\n| Data Element | Dev | QA | Staging |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| User accounts | Seeded via script | Seeded via script | Anonymised prod clone |\n| Payment credentials | Stripe test cards | Stripe test cards | Stripe test cards |\n| [Other element] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] |\n\n---\n\n6. SENSITIVE DATA HANDLING:\n| Field | C