nWinner: [Model name] — [Reason — metric + business constraint fit]\n\nStatistical significance:\n  - Test used: [e.g., McNemar test | Wilcoxon signed-rank | Bootstrap]\n  - Result: [e.g., p=0.003 — winner is significantly better than baseline at alpha=0.05]\n\nConfusion matrix / error analysis:\n  - Most common error type: [e.g., False negatives — missed churn cases]\n  - Root cause: [e.g., Low-activity users with no behavioural signal before churn]\n  - Mitigation: [e.g., Engineer recency features from last-30-day activity]\n\n---\n\n7. BIAS AND FAIRNESS:\nProtected attributes identified: [e.g., Age | Gender | Region | None identified]\n\nFairness metrics:\n| Group | Metric | Score | Threshold | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Age < 30] | [e.g., False positive rate] | [Score] | [e.g., <= 1.2x majority] | Pass / Fail |\n| [e.g., Age >= 30] | [e.g., False positive rate] | [Score] | [e.g., <= 1.2x majority] | Pass / Fail |\n\nBias found: Yes | No\nMitigation applied:\n  - [e.g., Reweighting training samples by demographic group]\n  - [e.g., Post-processing threshold adjustment per group]\n  - [e.g., Fairness constraint in objective function]\n\n---\n\n8. EXPLAINABILITY:\nMethod: SHAP | LIME | Integrated Gradients | Partial Dependence Plots | [Other]\nReason: [Why this method fits the model type and business requirement]\n\nGlobal feature importance (top 10):\n| Rank | Feature | Mean |SHAP| | Direction | Business Interpretation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [Feature] | [Value] | [Positive / Negative] | [What this means in business terms] |\n| 2 | [Feature] | [Value] | [Positive / Negative] | [Interpretation] |\n| 3 | [Feature] | [Value] | [Positive / Negative] | [Interpretation] |\n\nLocal explanation example:\n  - Prediction: [e.g., Churn probability = 0.87]\n  - Top drivers: [e.g., days_since_last_login=45 (+0.31) | support_tickets=3 (+0.18)]\n  - Counter-factors: [e.g., premium_plan=True (-0.12)]\n\nExplainability delivered to: [e.g., Business stakeholders via dashboard | Engineering via API]\n\n---\n\n9. EXPERIMENT TRACKING:\nTool: MLflow | Weights & Biases | Neptune | DVC | [Other]\nTracked per run:\n  - Parameters: [All hyperparameters]\n  - Metrics: [All evaluation metrics per fold and final]\n  - Artifacts: [Model file | Feature list | Preprocessing pipeline | SHAP plots]\n  - Environment: [Python version | Library versions | Random seeds]\n  - Dataset version: [Hash or version tag of training data]\n\nReproducibility checklist:\n  - [ ] Random seed fixed for all stochastic operations\n  - [ ] Dataset version pinned — not loaded from live source\n  - [ ] All preprocessing steps serialised in pipeline — not applied ad hoc\n  - [ ] Environment captured in requirements.txt or conda env file\n  - [ ] Experiment run ID logged alongside model artifact\n\n---\n\n10. PRODUCTIONISATION READINESS:\nModel serialisation: [e.g., joblib | pickle | ONNX | TorchScript | SavedModel]\nInference pipeline: [e.g., Preprocessing + model wrapped in sklearn Pipeline | FastAPI endpoint]\nInference latency: [e.g., p95 = 45ms on test hardware]\nMemory footprint: [e.g., 120MB loaded in memory]\n\nPre-deployment checklist:\n  - [ ] Model performance verified on held-out test set — not validation\n  - [ ] Inference pipeline tested end-to-end on production-like data\n  - [ ] Input schema validated — rejects malformed inputs gracefully\n  - [ ] Model version tagged and stored in model registry\n  - [ ] Rollback model identified and ready\n\nMonitoring plan:\n| Signal | Metric | Threshold | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prediction drift | PSI on score distribution | > 0.2 | Retrain trigger |\n| Feature drift | PSI per input feature | > 0.25 | Investigate + retrain |\n| Model performance | [Primary metric] on labelled prod sample | < [Threshold] | Retrain trigger |\n| Data quality | Null rate per feature | > 5% above baseline | Alert + investigate |\n\nRetraining strategy: [e.g., Scheduled monthly | Triggered by drift | Online learning]
61	17	Data analysis prompts	ROLE:\nYou are a senior Data Scientist with deep expertise in exploratory data analysis,\nstatistical analysis, hypothesis testing, and data-driven storytelling for\nbusiness and technical audiences.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nAnalysis Type: Exploratory data analysis | Statistical analysis | Hypothesis testing |\n              Cohort analysis | Funnel analysis | A/B test analysis |\n              Time series analysis | Segmentation | [Other]\nBusiness Question: [e.g., Why did revenue drop in Q3? | Which user segment churns most?]\nData Available: [e.g., 2M rows | 18 months | Tabular | Event logs | Survey]\nKey Metrics: [e.g., Revenue | Retention rate | Conversion rate | [Other]]\nAudience: Executive | Product team | Engineering | Data team | [Other]\nDecision to be made: [e.g., Whether to invest in feature X | Which segment to target]\nTools: Python (pandas, scipy, matplotlib) | R | SQL | Tableau | [Other]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the dataset and business question provided below, produce a complete\ndata analysis with findings, statistical validation, and actionable recommendations.\n\nYou MUST cover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Analysis plan — approach, methods, and questions to answer before touching data\n  2. Data quality assessment — completeness, consistency, validity, and anomaly detection\n  3. Univariate analysis — distribution of each key variable with interpretation\n  4. Bivariate and multivariate analysis — relationships, correlations, and interactions\n  5. Hypothesis testing — formal statistical tests with interpretation\n  6. Segmentation and cohort analysis — group-level patterns and differences\n  7. Trend and time series analysis — temporal patterns, seasonality, and anomalies\n  8. Key findings — ranked by business impact with supporting evidence\n  9. Recommendations — specific, actionable next steps tied to each finding\n  10. Limitations and caveats — what the data cannot tell us and confidence levels\n\nExamine the FULL dataset context before drawing any conclusion.\nEvery finding must be supported by a statistic, test result, or visual — not intuition.\nEvery recommendation must be tied to a specific finding — not generic advice.\nIf data is insufficient to answer the business question, flag as [NEEDS DATA] — never fabricate.\n\nDATASET AND BUSINESS QUESTION:\n[Paste dataset description, column definitions, summary statistics, known data issues,\nand the specific business question to be answered here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Every claim must cite a specific statistic or test result\n- Every hypothesis test must state H0, H1, test used, p-value, and conclusion\n- Confidence intervals must accompany every point estimate\n- Recommendations must be ranked by expected business impact\n- Limitations must be honest — do not overstate confidence in findings\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nDATA ANALYSIS REPORT\nProject: [Project Name] | Analysis Type: [Type]\nBusiness Question: [Question] | Decision: [Decision to be made]\nPrepared By: [Data Scientist Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\nAudience: [Audience]\n\n---\n\n1. ANALYSIS PLAN:\nBusiness question restated: [Precise, answerable version of the business question]\nSub-questions to answer:\n  1. [Sub-question 1]\n  2. [Sub-question 2]\n  3. [Sub-question 3]\n\nAnalytical approach:\n| Step | Method | Purpose | Tool |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [e.g., Data quality check] | [Purpose] | [e.g., pandas] |\n| 2 | [e.g., Distribution analysis] | [Purpose] | [e.g., matplotlib / seaborn] |\n| 3 | [e.g., Correlation analysis] | [Purpose] | [e.g., scipy] |\n| 4 | [e.g., Hypothesis test] | [Purpose] | [e.g., scipy.stats] |\n\nAssumptions:\n  - [Assumption 1 — e.g., Data is representative of the full user population]\n  - [Assumption 2 — e.g., No systematic bias in data collection]\n\n---\n\n2. DATA QUALITY ASSESSMENT:\nDataset overview:\n| Property | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Total rows | [N] |\n| Total columns | [N] |\n| Date range | [Start] to [End] |\n| Duplicates | [N rows — action taken] |\n| Memory usage | [e.g., 1.2GB] |\n\nColumn quality:\n| Column | Type | Missing % | Unique Values | Issues Found | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Column] | [Type] | [N%] | [N] | [e.g., Outliers | Invalid values | None] | [Action] |\n\nAnomalies detected:\n| Anomaly | Column | Description | Rows Affected | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Negative values] | [Column] | [Description] | [N] | [e.g., Exclude | Cap | Investigate] |\n| [e.g., Future dates] | [Column] | [Description] | [N] | [e.g., Remove — data entry error] |\n\nData quality score: [N/10] — [Overall assessment]\nImpact on analysis: [How data quality issues affect findings and confidence]\n\n---\n\n3. UNIVARIATE ANALYSIS:\nVARIABLE: [Variable name]\nType: Continuous | Categorical | Binary | DateTime\nDistribution: [e.g., Right-skewed | Normal | Bimodal | Uniform]\n\nSummary statistics:\n| Statistic | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Mean | [Value] |\n| Median | [Value] |\n| Std dev | [Value] |\n| Min | [Value] |\n| Max | [Value] |\n| 25th pct | [Value] |\n| 75th pct | [Value] |\n| Skewness | [Value] |\n| Kurtosis | [Value] |\n\nOutliers: [N outliers detected using [method] — [action taken]]\nBusiness interpretation: [What this distribution tells us about the business]\n\n---\n(repeat for every key variable)\n\n---\n\n4. BIVARIATE AND MULTIVARIATE ANALYSIS:\nRELATIONSHIP: [Variable A] vs [Variable B]\nMethod: [e.g., Pearson correlation | Spearman | Chi-square | ANOVA | Scatter plot]\nResult: [Statistic value — e.g., r = 0.72, p < 0.001]\nInterpretation: [What this relationship means in business terms]\nConfounders considered: [e.g., Controlled for user age and plan type]\n\nCorrelation matrix summary:\n| Variable Pair | Correlation | Strength | Direction | Business Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Var A] vs [Var B] | [r value] | Strong/Moderate/Weak | Positive/Negative | [Meaning] |\n| [Var A] vs [Var C] | [r value] | Strong/Moderate/Weak | Positive/Negative | [Meaning] |\n\nMultivariate finding: [Key interaction or pattern only visible when combining 3+ variables]\n\n---\n\n5. HYPOTHESIS TESTING:\nHYPOTHESIS [N]:\nBusiness question: [What business question this test answers]\nH0 (Null): [Precise null hypothesis]\nH1 (Alternative): [Precise alternative hypothesis]\nTest used: [e.g., Two-sample t-test | Mann-Whitney U | Chi-square | ANOVA | Z-test]\nReason for test: [Why this test fits the data type and distribution]\nSignificance level: alpha = [e.g., 0.05]\nSample sizes: n1 = [N] | n2 = [N]\nAssumptions checked: [e.g., Normality — Shapiro-Wilk p=0.12 — passes | Equal variance — Levene p=0.34 — passes]\n\nResults:\n| Statistic | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Test statistic | [Value] |\n| p-value | [Value] |\n| Effect size | [e.g., Cohen d = 0.42 — medium effect] |\n| 95% CI | [[Lower], [Upper]] |\n| Power | [e.g., 0.87 — adequate] |\n\nConclusion: [Reject H0 / Fail to reject H0] at alpha=[value]\nBusiness conclusion: [Plain English — what this means for the business decision]\n\n---\n(repeat for every hypothesis)\n\n---\n\n6. SEGMENTATION AND COHORT ANALYSIS:\nSegmentation variable: [e.g., User plan | Acquisition channel | Region | Cohort month]\n\nSegment comparison:\n| Segment | Size | [Metric 1] | [Metric 2] | [Metric 3] | Key Characteristic |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Segment A] | [N] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] | [What defines this segment] |\n| [Segment B] | [N] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] | [Characteristic] |\n| [Segment C] | [N] | [Value] | [Value] | [Value] | [Characteristic] |\n\nCohort analysis (if applicable):\n| Cohort | Size | Month 1 | Month 2 | Month 3 | Month 6 | Month 12 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Jan 2024] | [N] | [Retention %] | [%] | [%] | [%] | [%] |\n| [e.g., Feb 2024] | [N] | [%] | [%] | [%] | [%] | [%] |\n\nKey cohort insight: [Most important pattern — e.g., Month 3 drop-off is the critical churn point]\n\nStatistically significant segment differences:\n| Comparison | Metric | Test | p-value | Conclusion |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Seg A] vs [Seg B] | [Metric] | [Test] | [p] | [Significant / Not significant] |\n\n---\n\n7. TREND AND TIME SERIES ANALYSIS:\nPeriod analysed: [Start date] to [End date]\nGranularity: Daily | Weekly | Monthly | Quarterly\n\nTrend summary:\n| Metric | Overall Trend | Growth Rate | Seasonality | Anomalies |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Metric] | [Up/Down/Flat] | [e.g., +12% MoM] | [e.g., Weekly peak Fri-Sat] | [e.g., Drop week of 15 Mar] |\n\nAnomalies detected:\n| Date | Metric | Expected | Actual | Deviation | Likely Cause |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Date] | [Metric] | [Value] | [Value] | [e.g., -42%] | [e.g., Server outage | Campaign end | Holiday] |\n\nSeasonality: [Description of seasonal patterns and business implication]\nForecast (if applicable): [Next period estimate with confidence interval]\n\n---\n\n8. KEY FINDINGS:\nFINDING [N]:\nTitle: [Short descriptive title]\nInsight: [What the data shows — specific and evidence-backed]\nEvidence: [Statistic, test result, or visual reference supporting this finding]\nBusiness impact: High | Medium | Low\nConfidence: High | Medium | Low — [Reason for confidence level]\nImplication: [What this means for the business decision]\n\n---\n(repeat for every key finding — ranked High to Low impact)\n\n---\n\n9. RECOMMENDATIONS:\n| # | Recommendation | Finding it addresses | Expected Impact | Effort | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [Specific action] | Finding [N] | [e.g., +8% retention] | Low/Med/High | 🔴 Do first |\n| 2 | [Specific action] | Finding [N] | [Expected impact] | Low/Med/High | 🟡 Do next |\n| 3 | [Specific action] | Finding [N] | [Expected impact] | Low/Med/High | 🟢 Plan in |\n\nFurther analysis recommended:\n| Analysis | Question it answers | Data needed |\n|---|---|---|\n| [Analysis type] | [Question] | [Data required] |\n\n---\n\n10. LIMITATIONS AND CAVEATS:\n| Limitation | Impact on Findings | Confidence Adjustment |\n|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., 14% missing data on key variable] | [e.g., Retention analysis may be biased toward active users] | Reduce confidence to Medium |\n| [e.g., Only 3 months of data] | [e.g., Seasonality conclusions are preliminary] | Low confidence on seasonal findings |\n| [e.g., No control group] | [e.g., Cannot establish causation — correlation only] | Correlation finding only — not causal |\n| [e.g., Self-reported survey data] | [e.g., Social desirability bias possible] | Treat qualitative findings as directional |\n\nOverall confidence in findings: High | Medium | Low\nRecommended validation: [e.g., A/B test to confirm causal relationship | Collect 6 more months of data]
62	17	Insight generation support	ROLE:\nYou are a senior Data Scientist with deep expertise in translating complex data\nfindings into clear, compelling, and actionable insights for business and\ntechnical stakeholders across industries.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nInsight Type: Business performance | Customer behaviour | Product usage |\n              Operational efficiency | Market trends | Risk signals | [Other]\nSource Data: [e.g., Analytics events | CRM data | Financial reports | Survey | [Other]]\nAudience: C-suite | Product team | Marketing | Engineering | Operations | [Other]\nDecision to be made: [e.g., Budget allocation | Product roadmap | Campaign targeting]\nInsight Format: Executive narrative | Dashboard commentary | Slide deck | Report | [Other]\nUrgency: Strategic (long-term) | Tactical (short-term) | Operational (immediate)\n\nTASK:\nUsing the data findings and business context provided below, produce a complete\ninsight generation output that turns raw findings into clear business intelligence.\n\nYou MUST cover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Insight framing — translate data findings into business-relevant statements\n  2. So what analysis — why each finding matters and what it means for the business\n  3. Root cause narrative — what is driving the pattern, not just describing it\n  4. Opportunity identification — specific growth, efficiency, or risk opportunities\n  5. Risk signals — early warning indicators and what could go wrong\n  6. Audience-tailored narrative — right level of detail and language per audience\n  7. Prioritisation framework — which insights demand action first and why\n  8. Decision recommendations — specific choices the insight should drive\n  9. Confidence and uncertainty — how certain we are and what could change the picture\n  10. Next steps and follow-on questions — what to investigate next\n\nEvery insight must answer "So what?" — never just describe the data.\nEvery opportunity must be sized where data permits — not vague directional statements.\nEvery risk signal must include a recommended monitoring approach.\nIf data is insufficient to generate a confident insight, flag as [LOW CONFIDENCE] — be transparent.\n\nDATA FINDINGS AND BUSINESS CONTEXT:\n[Paste analysis results, key statistics, charts descriptions, trend summaries,\nand any business context relevant to the insight generation here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Insights must be written in plain language — no jargon for executive audience\n- Every insight must be tied to a specific data point — not general observation\n- Opportunity sizing must use data — not benchmark guesses\n- Risk signals must be specific — not generic warnings\n- Recommendations must be actionable within the stated decision timeframe\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nINSIGHT REPORT\nProject: [Project Name] | Insight Type: [Type]\nDecision: [Decision to be made] | Urgency: [Strategic | Tactical | Operational]\nPrepared By: [Data Scientist Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY]\nAudience: [Audience]\n\n---\n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY:\n[3-4 sentences — the single most important insight, its business implication,\nthe recommended action, and the confidence level]\nBottom line: [One sentence — the most important thing the reader must know]\n\n---\n\n1. INSIGHT FRAMING:\nINSIGHT [N]:\nData finding: [What the data shows — precise and specific]\nBusiness framing: [Same finding rewritten as a business statement]\nSo what: [Why this matters — business consequence if acted on or ignored]\nMagnitude: [e.g., This represents $2.4M in at-risk revenue | Affects 23% of users]\nConfidence: High | Medium | Low\nData source: [Which dataset, metric, and time period supports this]\n\n---\n(repeat for every insight)\n\n---\n\n2. SO WHAT ANALYSIS:\n| Insight | If we act | If we ignore | Urgency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Insight title] | [Expected positive outcome] | [Expected negative outcome] | High/Med/Low |\n| [Insight title] | [Expected outcome] | [Expected outcome] | High/Med/Low |\n\nMost critical insight to act on: [Title] — [One sentence reason]\n\n---\n\n3. ROOT CAUSE NARRATIVE:\nINSIGHT [N] ROOT CAUSE:\nObserved pattern: [What is happening]\nSurface cause: [The immediate driver — what changed]\nRoot cause: [The underlying reason the surface cause occurred]\nContributing factors:\n  - [Factor 1 — with supporting data]\n  - [Factor 2 — with supporting data]\nEvidence chain: [Data point A] -> [Data point B] -> [Conclusion]\nAlternative explanations considered:\n  - [Alternative 1 — ruled out because [evidence]]\n  - [Alternative 2 — ruled out because [evidence]]\nConfidence in root cause: High | Medium | Low — [Reason]\n\n---\n(repeat for every major insight)\n\n---\n\n4. OPPORTUNITY IDENTIFICATION:\nOPPORTUNITY [N]:\nTitle: [Short descriptive title]\nType: Revenue growth | Cost reduction | Efficiency gain | Risk mitigation | Retention | [Other]\nDescription: [What the opportunity is and where it comes from]\nData basis: [Specific finding that reveals this opportunity]\nSize estimate:\n  - Conservative: [e.g., +$800K ARR | -15% churn | +8% conversion]\n  - Base case: [e.g., +$1.4M ARR | -22% churn | +12% conversion]\n  - Optimistic: [e.g., +$2.1M ARR | -30% churn | +18% conversion]\nSizing methodology: [How the estimate was calculated]\nWhat needs to happen: [Specific actions required to capture this opportunity]\nOwner: [Team or role responsible]\nTimeline: [e.g., Actionable within Q2 | 6-month initiative | Immediate]\nEffort: Low | Medium | High\n\n---\n(repeat for every opportunity)\n\n---\n\n5. RISK SIGNALS:\nRISK [N]:\nSignal: [What the data is showing that suggests a risk]\nRisk description: [What could go wrong if this signal is ignored]\nAffected area: [e.g., Revenue | User retention | Operations | Compliance]\nEstimated impact: [e.g., Up to $1.2M revenue at risk | 18% of user base affected]\nProbability: High | Medium | Low — [Evidence basis]\nUrgency: Immediate | Short-term (1-3 months) | Medium-term (3-6 months)\nEarly warning indicator: [Specific metric to monitor that will confirm or deny the risk]\nMonitoring approach: [How to track this — metric | threshold | frequency | owner]\nRecommended pre-emptive action: [What to do now before the risk materialises]\n\n---\n(repeat for every risk signal)\n\n---\n\n6. AUDIENCE-TAILORED NARRATIVE:\nC-SUITE VERSION:\n[3-4 sentences — business impact only, no technical detail, dollar/percentage figures,\nclear recommendation, and confidence level. Written for someone with 30 seconds to read.]\n\nPRODUCT TEAM VERSION:\n[5-6 sentences — product and user behaviour focus, feature-level detail,\nuser segment breakdown, and specific product actions recommended.]\n\nENGINEERING / DATA TEAM VERSION:\n[Technical detail — data quality notes, methodology, metric definitions,\ncaveats, and follow-on analysis requirements.]\n\nMARKETING / OPERATIONS VERSION:\n[Campaign, segment, or operational focus — which users to target, which\nchannels to prioritise, and which operational changes to make.]\n\n---\n\n7. PRIORITISATION FRAMEWORK:\nPrioritisation criteria used:\n  1. Business impact — revenue, retention, or cost effect\n  2. Confidence level — strength of data evidence\n  3. Actionability — can a decision be made with current information\n  4. Urgency — time sensitivity of the opportunity or risk\n\nPrioritised insight matrix:\n| Insight | Business Impact | Confidence | Actionable Now | Urgency | Priority Score | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Insight title] | High/Med/Low | High/Med/Low | Yes/No | High/Med/Low | [1-10] | 🔴 Act now |\n| [Insight title] | High/Med/Low | High/Med/Low | Yes/No | High/Med/Low | [1-10] | 🟡 Plan next |\n| [Insight title] | High/Med/Low | High/Med/Low | Yes/No | High/Med/Low | [1-10] | 🟢 Monitor |\n| [Insight title] | High/Med/Low | High/Med/Low | Yes/No | High/Med/Low | [1-10] | ⚪ Defer |\n\nTop priority: [Insight title] — [One sentence justification]\n\n---\n\n8. DECISION RECOMMENDATIONS:\nDECISION [N]:\nDecision to be made: [Specific decision this insight should drive]\nRecommended choice: [Clear recommendation — not "it depends"]\nData basis: [Specific findings that support this recommendation]\nExpected outcome: [What we expect to happen if this decision is made]\nRisk of this choice: [Downside if the recommendation is wrong]\nAlternative considered: [What else was considered and why it was rejected]\nDecision owner: [Who needs to make this decision]\nDeadline: [When this decision needs to be made to capture the opportunity]\nSuccess metric: [How we will know the decision was the right one]\n\n---\n(repeat for every decision)\n\n---\n\n9. CONFIDENCE AND UNCERTAINTY:\nOverall confidence in insights: High | Medium | Low\n\nConfidence by insight:\n| Insight | Confidence | Key Uncertainty | What would change the picture |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Insight title] | High/Med/Low | [Main source of uncertainty] | [e.g., If Q4 data shows reversal] |\n| [Insight title] | High/Med/Low | [Uncertainty] | [What would change it] |\n\nScenarios that could invalidate findings:\n| Scenario | Likelihood | Impact on Findings | Monitoring |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Macro downturn changes user behaviour] | Low/Med/High | [e.g., Churn model assumptions break] | [Monthly cohort review] |\n| [e.g., Competitor launches competing feature] | Low/Med/High | [Impact] | [Monitoring approach] |\n\nConfidence-adjusted recommendations:\n  - High confidence findings: Recommend immediate action\n  - Medium confidence findings: Recommend targeted test or pilot before full commitment\n  - Low confidence findings: Recommend further data collection before action\n\n---\n\n10. NEXT STEPS AND FOLLOW-ON QUESTIONS:\nImmediate actions (this week):\n| Action | Owner | Purpose | Deadline |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Share report with product lead] | [Owner] | [Purpose] | [Date] |\n| [e.g., Set up monitoring dashboard for risk signal] | [Owner] | [Purpose] | [Date] |\n\nFollow-on analysis required:\n| Question | Why it matters | Analysis needed | Data required | Timeline |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Question] | [Business value] | [Analysis type] | [Data needed] | [e.g., 1 week] |\n\nHypotheses to test:\n| Hypothesis | Test method | Success metric | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [e.g., Reducing onboarding steps increases activation] | [A/B test] | [+5% activation rate] | [Product team] |\n\nReview cadence: [e.g., Weekly insight refresh | Monthly deep dive | Ad hoc on trigger]
